__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){Object.defineProperty(e,"__esModule",{value:!0}),e.phaseMap=e.phaseData=void 0;e.phaseData={Menstrual:{name:'Menstrual Phase',description:'Your body is working hard to renew itself as hormones reach their lowest levels.',hormoneInfo:'Hormones estrogen and progesterone are at their lowest',tags:['menstrual phase','period','cycle days 1-5 but can range 3-8 days'],irregularMessages:["If your period hasn't started yet, be patient with your body. Cycles can vary, and you are doing your best.",'Reach out for support if you need it. Sharing how you feel is a sign of strength.','Comfort foods, cozy clothes, and a favorite show can be small acts of self-kindness today.',"Trust your body's wisdom. Each cycle is a new beginning, and you are resilient."],messages:{sensitive:['Your body is working hard to renew itself as hormones reach their lowest-give yourself credit for making it through another cycle, and notice one thing you handled well, even if it was just getting through a tough day.',"As the fog lifts, take a moment to reflect: What helped you cope last phase? Is there a small adjustment you'd like to try next time?",'The start of your period is a natural reset. Consider sharing your reflections with someone close. An honest conversation can help mend any relationship bumps from last week.','If things got rocky in your relationships, now is a time to reconnect. A simple "thank you for your patience" or "I\'m sorry for how I acted last week" can be a powerful reset.',"If arguments or tension flared when symptoms were at their peak, now's a good time to reflect! Are there any important conversations you need to have?","Even if the last cycle felt overwhelming, notice one small moment you're proud of. Maybe you paused before reacting and reached out for support. That's progress.","Your body is shedding and rebuilding; you can do the same emotionally. What's one thing you'd like to leave behind from the last premenstrual phase, and one thing you want to carry forward?","If your symptoms were intense, forgive yourself for what was out of your control. Use today's clarity to jot down a plan for next time.",'This is your moment to reset. Reflect, repair, and remind yourself: you are growing stronger with every cycle.',"Each cycle is a new chance to learn about yourself - what worked, what didn't, and how you can support yourself and your relationships even better next time.","If you're feeling relief as symptoms fade, take a moment to notice what you want to remember for next month. Write it down or share it with someone you trust.",'You survived another round of hormonal storms. Take pride in your resilience and use this time to gently plan for a smoother next phase.'],calm:['If cramps or aches are making you irritable, try a warm bath or a heating pad for comfort.','Gentle self-care, like making a favorite drink or reading, can help lift your mood.','As your body rests and renews during your period, let yourself gently review the past phase. No judgment, just curiosity.','Hormones are low, and you may feel tired or sore. Let yourself slow down and ask: What helped you get through the tough days last time?','If you had an argument or felt distance in a relationship, now is a time to reconnect with a kind message or quiet gesture.','If you made mistakes in the last phase, offer yourself forgiveness. Everyone has hard days. What matters is how you care for yourself now.','Did you find any small comfort that soothed you in this past premenstrual phase? Keep that in your toolkit for next time.','If you struggled with plans that were too ambitious, consider scheduling smaller, more manageable activities for your next symptomatic phase.','Notice if certain days or situations were especially hard. Block off "recovery time" in your calendar for next month.',"If you're still feeling aches or fatigue, rest is not laziness, it's part of your healing. Let others know you need a little extra care.",'If you apologized or made repairs in your relationships, let that be a reminder that connection can be rebuilt, even after tough moments.','Use this phase to gently check in with loved ones. Sometimes a quiet "I appreciate you" is all it takes to mend a rough patch.',"If you're still feeling the after-effects of your symptoms, give yourself permission to do less and move slowly.","Treat yourself with the same patience and kindness you'd offer a friend. Healing and learning take time."],humor:["Your hormones have left for a vacation. If you're grumpy, treat yourself to a snack and a nap.",'Cramps and mood swings? Your uterus is doing its monthly home renovation: pajamas are the dress code.','Your hormones are at rock bottom and your uterus is on "spring cleaning" duty; if last week was a disaster, consider this your official reset button.','If you survived the emotional rollercoaster (and cramps), reward yourself with your favorite snack and a guilt-free nap. Future you will thank you.','Relationship drama last week? Now\'s the time for a peace offering-maybe a "sorry for being a hormonal tornado" note or just sharing the last cookie.','Did your plans for self-care last cycle flop? Next time, schedule "bare minimum" days and call it "advanced self-care."','If you snapped at someone, now\'s a good time for a gentle check-in: "Sorry, my hormones were driving last week. Let\'s hit refresh?"','Cramps still hanging around? Declare today a "blanket burrito" day. Chores can wait; comfort comes first.','If you forgot every coping skill in the book, add a sticky note to your fridge for next time: "Try snacks before snark."','If you\'re still feeling tender, let your loved ones know: "Handle with care. Uterus under construction."',"If you apologized for last week's outburst, consider it a relationship deposit. Small gestures add up."]}},Follicular:{name:'Follicular Phase',description:'Estrogen is rising and motivation may return. This is a great time for new projects.',hormoneInfo:'Estrogen rising, progesterone low',tags:['follicular phase','after period to ovulation'],messages:{sensitive:["Estrogen is rising-if you're feeling hopeful, set a small goal for yourself today.","As your body rebuilds, motivation may return. If you're restless, try starting a new project or hobby.",'If anxiety lingers, channel it into movement; go for a walk or stretch!','Feeling more social? Reach out to a friend and make plans, even if brief.','If self-doubt appears, write down a recent accomplishment to remind yourself of your strengths.','Noticing your mood lift? Celebrate by doing something you enjoy, like listening to upbeat music.',"If you're still low, take one small step toward something that brings you joy.",'As your body prepares for ovulation, try something creative or playful.','If you feel optimistic, use that energy to organize or plan for the week ahead.','When anxiety pops up, pause and focus on something in your environment that makes you smile.',"If you're eager for change, start with a small, manageable task and build from there.",'Let this phase be a fresh start-embrace the new energy as it comes.',"Use the calendar to flag days when you're most likely to feel irritable or low. Planning ahead is an act of strength.",'Schedule your go-to soothing activities right in the calendar for your toughest days.','For your next symptomatic phase, check your calendar for days that are hardest, block off time for rest or gentle activities.','Add a note in the calendar for your support person to check in with you on high-risk days.','Each entry you make is a step toward an empowering cycle, tailored just for you.'],calm:['With estrogen gently rising, you may feel lighter. Allow yourself to move at your own pace.','If energy returns, use it for something that feels good, like a gentle walk or favorite activity.','Feeling optimistic? Let yourself enjoy the moment, no pressure to do more.','If anxiety is still present, find comfort in a familiar routine or soothing ritual.','When motivation is low, choose one enjoyable thing to do, no matter how small.',"If you're still tired, rest is still important-listen to your body.",'As your body prepares for ovulation, enjoy connecting with others if you feel up to it.','If sadness lingers, reach out for support or spend time in a comforting space.',"If you're feeling hopeful, let it guide your choices for the day.",'Notice small positive changes and acknowledge them with kindness.','Nurture yourself as you transition into this new phase.','Plan ahead by scheduling a calming activity for days when you tend to feel overwhelmed.','See within your calendar to block off time for comfort routines-like a warm bath or favorite show-on challenging days.','Gently prompt yourself to review your plans before your most vulnerable days.','Plan a small act of kindness for yourself in the app for each day you expect to feel low.'],humor:["Estrogen's back and ready to party! If you're suddenly reorganizing your closet, go with it.",'Feeling chatty? Your hormones are sending out friend requests-accept as many as you like.',"If you're extra motivated, thank your rising estrogen-it's your personal cheerleader.","Noticing more energy? Your body's hosting a pep rally-join in or just wave from the sidelines.","If you're still tired, tell your hormones to grab a coffee-no shame in a nap.",'Mood lifting? That\'s your cycle\'s way of sending you a "you got this" memo.',"If you're feeling creative, your ovaries are running a Pinterest board-try a new craft.","Not sure what phase you're in? If you're less cranky and more chatty, follicular is your jam.",'If anxiety pops up, picture your hormones doing yoga-deep breaths help.',"If you're still in low gear, even your hormones need a warm-up lap.",'When in doubt, treat yourself to a "just because" celebration-your body\'s working hard.','Planning for next symptomatic phase? Set a reminder: "Be kind to self, stock up on chocolate, warn partner in advance."','Planning for next symptomatic phase? Schedule "bare minimum days" in the calendar! Pants optional, kindness required.','Planning for next symptomatic phase? Mark your upcoming high-risk days in your calendar as "Handle with Care" and add a reminder to stock up on chocolate.']}},Ovulation:{name:'Ovulation Phase',description:'You may feel more social and energetic. This is your most fertile time.',hormoneInfo:'Estrogen peaks, progesterone is low and a slight rise in testosterone',tags:['ovulation','10-14 days before the next period'],messages:{sensitive:["Estrogen peaks and LH surges-if you're confident, use this time to tackle something important.",'High energy and social vibes are common; connect with someone who lifts you up.','If mood swings appear, pause and step outside for a few deep breaths.','Feeling anxious? Channel that energy into a favorite activity or quick walk.',"If you're sensitive, take a break and focus on something that brings you comfort.",'Noticing increased libido or creativity? Embrace it with an activity that excites you.',"If you're overwhelmed, step back and listen to calming music.",'Feeling powerful? Channel that energy into a goal or creative project.','If irritability sneaks in, take a few minutes alone to reset.',"Celebrate your body's strength and wisdom with a small act of self-kindness.","If you're feeling scattered, pick one thing to focus on and see it through.",'Use this time to honor your unique rhythm and capacity for growth.'],calm:["As ovulation arrives, estrogen is at its highest-enjoy this natural lift if it's there.",'Confidence and sociability may increase; connect with others if it feels good.','If mood swings happen, be gentle and give yourself a moment to regroup.','Feeling anxious? Take slow breaths and notice the present moment.',"If you're extra sensitive, treat yourself with patience and calm.",'Noticing strong emotions? Observe them and do something soothing, like sipping tea.','If you feel scattered, focus on one thing at a time.','Embrace any surge in creativity or motivation; use it for something joyful.','If irritability arises, take a short walk or listen to calming sounds.','Hormones are shifting quickly; rest when you need to.',"If you're overwhelmed, reach out to someone who understands.","Trust your body's process and honor your needs."],humor:["Estrogen's throwing a rooftop party-if you're feeling like the main character, enjoy your spotlight.",'Ovulation: when your hormones are basically your personal hype crew.',"If you're extra flirty or creative, thank your body for the VIP treatment.",'Mood swings? Your hormones are just showing off their dance moves-try not to step on any toes.',"If you're feeling powerful, use it! If not, blame the hormones for false advertising.","Noticing a twinge? That's your ovary waving hello-no RSVP required.","If you're suddenly the life of the party, your hormones are working overtime.",'Feeling scattered? Your hormones are running a group chat. Mute notifications if needed.',"If you're overwhelmed, imagine your hormones taking a bow, applause optional.","Confidence up, motivation up, hair cooperating, ovulation's got your back.",'Treat yourself to a "main event" snack. Your body\'s hosting a cycle celebration.']}},Luteal:{name:'Luteal Phase',description:'You might experience some PMS symptoms. Focus on self-care and gentle activities.',hormoneInfo:'Progesterone rises and then drops; estrogen drops initially and begins to rise to a short peak and then drops again',tags:['luteal phase','symptomatic phase','symptoms','post-ovulation to menstruation','12-14 days long','premenstrual'],messages:{sensitive:["Progesterone rises and estrogen falls-if you're irritable, take a break and do something that calms you.",'Mood swings or anxiety are common; plan a simple pleasure for yourself today.','If sadness hits, schedule a small activity that usually lifts your mood.','Feeling overwhelmed? Step away for a few minutes and focus on something grounding, like your breath.','If anger flares, choose a calming distraction, like a favorite podcast or walk.','Noticing cravings or fatigue? Nourish yourself with a comforting snack and rest.',"If you're anxious, write down your thoughts and look for a more balanced perspective.",'Feeling disconnected? Reach out to someone for a quick chat or comforting message.',"If you're restless, try a creative or physical activity to channel your energy.",'Remember: these symptoms are temporary, give yourself credit for getting through each day.',"If hopelessness appears, remind yourself of past cycles and how you've managed before.",'Use this time to practice self-care and celebrate your resilience.','As your body works hard, irritability can rise. Let loved ones know you need some quiet time to recharge.','Feeling withdrawn? Give yourself permission to rest and plan a gentle activity for later in the week.',"If you're overwhelmed, break your day into tiny steps and celebrate each one you finish.",'When energy is low, ask for help with chores or order takeout! Your well-being comes first.',"If you're feeling invisible, share your needs with someone close; your voice matters, even on tough days.",'Tears flowing easily? Let yourself cry, then treat yourself kindly, like you would a friend.','If irritability spikes, step away for a few minutes and listen to calming music before returning to the conversation.','If anger is making you snap at everyone, give yourself grace. Important conversations can wait for a bit. Breathe or hold an ice pack.','Overwhelmed by pain or emotion? Wrap yourself in a warm blanket and watch a comfort show.',"If arguments or tension flare when symptoms are at their peak, remember that it's ok to table the conversation now and address it when you feel better.","If you're missing feeling the connection, invite someone for a low-key chat or a shared cup of tea.",'Struggling to get moving? Choose one small, manageable task and give yourself credit for completing it.','When hopelessness creeps in, remind yourself this phase will pass and plan something to look forward to next week.','When you feel tension rising, pause and send a caring text or note to your partner. Even a small gesture can help maintain connection.',"If you're tempted to start a conversation with criticism, begin with a thank you or something you appreciate about them.","When you're feeling rejected or distant, reach out with a gentle bid for connection, like asking for a hug or sharing a funny meme.","If you notice you're withdrawing, plan a short check-in with your loved ones, even five minutes together can strengthen your bond.",'When anger flares, step away for a few minutes and return when you feel calmer, then share what you need in a softer tone.',"Notice when your partner tries to connect (a smile, a question, a touch) and do your best to respond, even if it's brief.","If you regret something you said, acknowledge it and share what you'll try next time to avoid repeating it."],calm:['As progesterone rises, mood swings and fatigue may appear-respond with patience and gentle care.',"If you're irritable or anxious, give yourself space to cool off.",'Feeling sad? Do something kind for yourself, like making your favorite drink.','If anger or frustration builds, take a few moments alone to reset.','Cravings and low motivation are common; let yourself enjoy a comforting treat.',"If you're overwhelmed, focus on one thing at a time and let go of the rest.",'Feeling disconnected? Spend time with someone who makes you feel safe.','If anxiety is high, try a calming activity, like gentle stretching or listening to music.','Mood swings can be intense; remind yourself that these feelings will pass.','If you feel hopeless, rest and remember that brighter days are ahead.','Hormone shifts can affect sleep and appetite; adjust your routine as needed.',"Treat yourself with the same compassion you'd offer a friend.",'Feeling withdrawn? Give yourself space for quiet time and gentle activities.','If your mood is low, light a candle or play calming music to create a soothing atmosphere.','When sadness lingers, reach out to someone you trust for a gentle conversation.',"If you're overwhelmed, break your day into tiny steps and rest between them.",'On days when energy is scarce, let yourself nap or simply do less.','If irritability is high, step outside and notice the world around you for a few moments.','When motivation is missing, choose the easiest task and let that be enough.',"If you're feeling sensitive, write down your thoughts or emotions to help process them.","If you're anxious, try focusing on your breath or holding something soft in your hands.",'Be gentle with yourself, your body is asking for care.',"If emotions are running high, let your partner know you're having a tough day and appreciate their patience.",'When you feel distant, invite your partner to sit with you quietly or go for a short walk together.',"If you're too tired for conversation, offer a gentle touch or smile to show you still care.","When you sense a conflict brewing, suggest taking a break and revisiting the topic when you're both calmer.","If you're sensitive to jokes or comments, let your partner know gently and ask for extra kindness.",'After a rough day, end the night with a small gesture of connection, like a goodnight kiss or holding hands.'],humor:['Progesterone\'s in charge and snacks are your new best friend-if you\'re cranky, blame the "luteal lounge."','Mood swings? Your hormones are just rehearsing for their next big drama.','If you\'re feeling irritable, take a "timeout" before you text your ex or yell at your toaster.',"Cravings for chocolate? Your body's just following doctor's orders-sort of.","If you're tired and grumpy, your hormones are running a marathon-let them nap, and join in.",'Feeling like a detective in your own mood mystery? Track symptoms for clues.',"If your mood changes faster than a playlist, remember: you're still the DJ.",'Anger flaring? Imagine your hormones as rowdy toddlers. Sometimes you just need a timeout before you play.',"If you're questioning every decision, your hormones are just running a Q&A session.","Bloating, cravings, and irritability? Your body's prepping for menstruation like it's stocking up for winter.","If you're not yourself, your hormones are just trying on new personalities. Hang tight!","When in doubt, treat yourself to a cozy night in-your body's earned it.",'If commercials make you cry, remember: your hormones are just practicing for the Oscars.','Low energy? Even your estrogen hit snooze-curl up and let the world wait.',"If you're annoyed by everyone, try hiding with a good book and let the world fend for itself.",'Feeling dramatic? Blame the hormone drop and have a solo karaoke session in your living room.','If motivation is missing, reward yourself for just getting out of bed-gold star earned.',"Tears flowing? Your body's just watering your inner garden. Grab tissues and let it rain.",'If your mood\'s swinging, treat yourself to your favorite comfort show and call it "research."','When irritability spikes, take a "timeout". Even superheroes need a break from saving the day.',"If you're feeling snacky, remember: it's not a craving, it's a survival strategy.",'When in doubt, declare today a "do-nothing" day-your hormones will thank you.',"When you feel like yelling, send a meme instead-it's safer for both of you.",'If you\'re grumpy, announce a "hug coupon" redeemable when you\'re ready-no expiration date.','If you\'re feeling distant, invite your partner to a "silent staring contest". Winner gets to pick the next show.','When you need space, say "I\'m going to my blanket fort. Entry by invitation only."','If you regret an outburst, offer a peace offering: "Sorry for my dragon roar. Can we share some ice cream?"',"When you're too tired to talk, send a GIF that says it all (bonus points for cats or sloths).",'If you\'re sensitive to jokes, declare a "no sarcasm zone" for the evening and enforce with silly hats.','When you need help, try: "Can you be my sidekick today? Superheroes need backup."','If you\'re feeling misunderstood, write your partner a "PMDD survival guide" with tips and a coupon for future patience.','After a tough day, suggest a "mutual grumble session"- five minutes to complain, then switch to something fun.']}}},e.phaseMap={'#EF5B70':'Menstrual','#F4B740':'Ovulation','#049299':'Luteal','#7C77B9':'Follicular','#FCE8EB':'Menstrual','#FFF4D8':'Ovulation','#DDF7F4':'Luteal','#C5C1F5':'Follicular'}},3600,[]);
